WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.172
Every click in Power Query writes code for you.

2
00:00:04.172 --> 00:00:09.417
In this lesson, you will learn to open the Advanced Editor, read that code,

3
00:00:09.417 --> 00:00:16.160
copy cleaning steps between queries to save time, and fix the most common syntax errors. Ready? Let's go!

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.005
Every step in Power Query is written in a language called M.

5
00:00:22.005 --> 00:00:26.344
Here is the code for a second copy of our Financial Sample query.

6
00:00:26.344 --> 00:00:28.680
It has only two steps so far.

7
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.038
This copy holds raw data, while the original query already has several cleaning steps.

8
00:00:45.038 --> 00:00:49.700
Instead of repeating them by hand, let's copy the code. Work smart!

9
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.380
I open the Advanced Editor for the original query, select the cleaning steps, and copy them.

10
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.000
Then I paste them into the second query, right after its existing steps.

11
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.793
See the message at the bottom: Token Identifier expected. M is strict.

12
00:01:29.793 --> 00:01:34.686
Every step ends with a comma, except the last one, and the word in

13
00:01:34.686 --> 00:01:38.180
must be followed by the name of the final step.

14
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.080
So I correct the step names, and point the line after in to the last step.

15
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.004
Another common message: Token Eof expected.

16
00:02:25.004 --> 00:02:30.614
It usually means extra text after the final step, or a missing comma earlier.

17
00:02:30.614 --> 00:02:34.220
Read the message, check the line, and fix it.

18
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.042
No syntax errors detected.

19
00:02:47.042 --> 00:02:51.730
Click Done, and both queries now share the same cleaning steps. Pro tip:

20
00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:57.860
copy your code into a text file before you edit it, so you can always go back.

